WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.275 --> 00:00:02.254
- You try harder and harder
to kind of fit into a mold,

00:00:02.254 --> 00:00:03.289
and I think what they don't realize

00:00:03.289 --> 00:00:04.401
is that you're the only one that's you.

00:00:04.401 --> 00:00:07.166
I tend to overthink things until

00:00:07.166 --> 00:00:08.779
the idea becomes pointless.

00:00:08.779 --> 00:00:10.973
If you feel like what you're
doing is becoming a formula,

00:00:10.973 --> 00:00:12.043
break the formula.

00:00:12.043 --> 00:00:14.959
He's an American singer,
actor, and producer.

00:00:14.959 --> 00:00:18.603
He was the USA's best selling
recording artist in 2007.

00:00:18.603 --> 00:00:21.556
To date, he's sold over
25 million records.

00:00:21.556 --> 00:00:22.427
He's Josh Groban,

00:00:22.427 --> 00:00:25.166
and here's my take on his
top 10 rules of success.

00:00:25.166 --> 00:00:26.755
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:26.755 --> 00:00:28.997
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best,

00:00:28.997 --> 00:00:31.114
and as always, guys, as
your watching the videos,

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:33.438
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:33.438 --> 00:00:35.128
please leave it down the comments below

00:00:35.128 --> 00:00:36.593
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:36.593 --> 00:00:38.011
can be inspired as well.

00:00:38.011 --> 00:00:40.062
Also, as you are writing something down,

00:00:40.062 --> 00:00:43.116
it's much more likely to
stick in your head, too.

00:00:43.116 --> 00:00:43.949
Enjoy!

00:00:45.048 --> 00:00:47.111
(booming)

00:00:47.111 --> 00:00:50.361
(inspirational melody)

00:00:57.215 --> 00:00:58.412
- People that I really look up to

00:00:58.412 --> 00:01:00.200
are those people that kind
of did it their own way.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.000
Kind of people that
let people come to them

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.231
and ignored the hype, and just went ahead

00:01:06.231 --> 00:01:08.699
and tried to come up with their
own personality musically.

00:01:08.699 --> 00:01:10.905
It's a little bit of a harder path,

00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:13.251
especially with the way
that the industry is now,

00:01:13.251 --> 00:01:16.590
but I think that ultimately,
if I can be looked at

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.671
not as a genre, but as, oh I just like,

00:01:18.671 --> 00:01:20.381
it's just Josh, I like Josh, you know?

00:01:20.381 --> 00:01:21.642
Then, that would be a great goal for me.

00:01:21.642 --> 00:01:24.574
If I can look back, and
hopefully have a long career

00:01:24.574 --> 00:01:26.717
and look back and say
that I did everything

00:01:26.717 --> 00:01:29.959
the way I wanted to, and just
kind of stayed true to myself.

00:01:29.959 --> 00:01:31.493
That's the one thing I
think most new artists

00:01:31.493 --> 00:01:32.826
need to realize.

00:01:33.669 --> 00:01:36.114
That you try harder and harder
to kind of fit into a mold,

00:01:36.114 --> 00:01:37.227
and I think that what they don't realize

00:01:37.227 --> 00:01:38.761
is that you're the only
one that's you, you know?

00:01:38.761 --> 00:01:41.188
If you just embrace that 100%,

00:01:41.188 --> 00:01:42.982
you'd be the most unique thing out there,

00:01:42.982 --> 00:01:44.226
because there's nothing else like that.

00:01:44.226 --> 00:01:47.626
So, it's hard to do, but if
you can keep that mentality,

00:01:47.626 --> 00:01:49.768
I'd like to try to do that.

00:01:49.768 --> 00:01:52.223
Anybody that says that they
had their big break, you know?

00:01:52.223 --> 00:01:54.018
It winds up being because

00:01:54.018 --> 00:01:56.088
you're in the right
place at the right time,

00:01:56.088 --> 00:01:58.486
but it's the preparation
meets the opportunity.

00:01:58.486 --> 00:02:01.267
So, in a lot of times, that opportunity,

00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:02.738
you don't know that that's the opportunity

00:02:02.738 --> 00:02:03.571
when it happens, you know?

00:02:03.571 --> 00:02:06.516
For me, meeting David, singing
with Celine that night,

00:02:06.516 --> 00:02:08.732
I originally said, "I
don't think I can do this.

00:02:08.732 --> 00:02:10.356
"Thank you, but no thank you.",

00:02:10.356 --> 00:02:11.776
'cause you know.
- You were scared.

00:02:11.776 --> 00:02:12.763
- I was scared, I was terrified.

00:02:12.763 --> 00:02:13.723
I was 17 years old,

00:02:13.723 --> 00:02:14.641
and I had to fill in the shoes

00:02:14.641 --> 00:02:17.137
of a 45 year-old opera
superstar, you know,

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:18.702
and I was thinkin' to myself,

00:02:18.702 --> 00:02:22.147
well, I just don't want to mess up, so.

00:02:22.147 --> 00:02:23.864
He called me back and he said,
"No, you don't understand,

00:02:23.864 --> 00:02:24.957
"I'm not asking you.

00:02:24.957 --> 00:02:27.991
"You're not going to let me
down, get your butt over here.",

00:02:27.991 --> 00:02:30.516
and so I went, and that
was one of the first things

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:31.349
that really taught me

00:02:31.349 --> 00:02:33.188
that it's about really
throwing yourself in the fire

00:02:33.188 --> 00:02:34.133
and taking those risks.

00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:37.314
So, to a certain extent,
it's been a huge rise,

00:02:37.314 --> 00:02:39.037
but at the same time, I still
very much feel like a student

00:02:39.037 --> 00:02:41.338
of it, and I'm learning
every step of the way.

00:02:41.338 --> 00:02:43.116
- So, the album was out there,

00:02:43.116 --> 00:02:45.730
and it started to gain some momentum, or

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:46.581
what started to change?

00:02:46.581 --> 00:02:48.803
- I don't know, because I was
going to Virgin Megastores

00:02:48.803 --> 00:02:52.194
when they still existed,
and putting on sunglasses,

00:02:52.194 --> 00:02:54.347
not that I needed them at
the time, and kind of say,

00:02:54.347 --> 00:02:56.162
"Do you have that Josh Groban CD?".

00:02:56.162 --> 00:02:57.487
(audience laughing)

00:02:57.487 --> 00:02:59.610
You know, "I'm looking for it everywhere,

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.960
"I just can't find it, what a
lovely young voice he has.",

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.534
(audience laughing)

00:03:04.534 --> 00:03:06.386
and they're like, "Who's
that, Josh Brolin?

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:07.219
"I didn't know he sang!".

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:09.321
(audience laughing)

00:03:09.321 --> 00:03:11.238
And so, they would have to pull,

00:03:11.238 --> 00:03:13.706
I'd keep finding that
they would have to pull

00:03:13.706 --> 00:03:16.440
it out of storage from way,
way down in the depths of--

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.983
- Right on the corner.
- Yeah, exactly, yeah.

00:03:17.983 --> 00:03:19.693
- Bastards!
- It was Bed, Bath &amp; Beyond.

00:03:19.693 --> 00:03:20.733
- I was in the beyond section,

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:24.068
I was like way down, this will
never sell section, you know?

00:03:24.068 --> 00:03:25.393
So, they would pull one
out for me, and I was like,

00:03:25.393 --> 00:03:27.931
"Oh, excellent, you really
should put these out in front.",

00:03:27.931 --> 00:03:30.364
and so it went from
that to, and I had done

00:03:30.364 --> 00:03:31.197
a number of different things.

00:03:31.197 --> 00:03:32.933
I'd sung in the Olympics,

00:03:32.933 --> 00:03:34.241
I'd been on the Rosie O'Donnell show,

00:03:34.241 --> 00:03:35.979
who was very supportive early on.

00:03:35.979 --> 00:03:40.146
It wasn't until 20/20 wound
up doing a story about me.

00:03:43.077 --> 00:03:44.285
I think primarily based on

00:03:44.285 --> 00:03:47.815
a wonderful New York Times
piece, actually, that came out,

00:03:47.815 --> 00:03:50.535
where you guys kind of wrote

00:03:50.535 --> 00:03:52.583
a really wonderful article on,

00:03:52.583 --> 00:03:54.751
and they said we want to do a story,

00:03:54.751 --> 00:03:57.011
want to interview you, we
want to connect the dots.

00:03:57.011 --> 00:03:59.125
So what they did was they basically said

00:03:59.125 --> 00:04:01.736
to that 20/20 audience,
you've seen him here, here,

00:04:01.736 --> 00:04:02.897
here, and here.

00:04:02.897 --> 00:04:04.430
Here's where you can ask for his CD,

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:06.283
and here's his name and here's his face,

00:04:06.283 --> 00:04:07.116
and here's his hair.

00:04:07.116 --> 00:04:09.019
(audience laughing)

00:04:09.019 --> 00:04:12.846
That was the lightbulb that
the country kind of turned on,

00:04:12.846 --> 00:04:15.962
and I went from being 148
on the charts the week prior

00:04:15.962 --> 00:04:17.760
to being number eight the next week.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.141
It was that fast.
- Wow.

00:04:19.141 --> 00:04:21.727
And Oprah followed, and
it was all she wrote.

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:23.553
- [Female Interviewer] Are
you critical about your music?

00:04:23.553 --> 00:04:24.792
- I'm pretty hypercritical.

00:04:24.792 --> 00:04:27.272
I'm really hypercritical about it.

00:04:27.272 --> 00:04:28.310
I tend to overthink things

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:31.060
until the idea becomes pointless.

00:04:32.099 --> 00:04:34.413
So, one of the other
things that I've learned

00:04:34.413 --> 00:04:36.414
in my three album career,

00:04:36.414 --> 00:04:37.733
(laughs)

00:04:37.733 --> 00:04:39.533
- A long career.
- Yeah, exactly, right.

00:04:39.533 --> 00:04:41.114
- Is to let things go.

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:42.616
Sometimes, we just need to let them,

00:04:42.616 --> 00:04:43.858
let the child out into the world.

00:04:43.858 --> 00:04:46.925
- [Australian Interviewer]
When you go in to write tracks,

00:04:46.925 --> 00:04:49.195
is it a process where
it just flows naturally,

00:04:49.195 --> 00:04:50.028
or do you sit down,

00:04:50.028 --> 00:04:51.480
well this is the type of song I want,

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.469
this is the type of people
I want it to connect to.

00:04:53.469 --> 00:04:55.211
- Yeah, I think it's kind of hard,

00:04:55.211 --> 00:04:57.663
it's very hard to generalize
what type of audience

00:04:57.663 --> 00:04:59.442
I'd want to connect with.

00:04:59.442 --> 00:05:02.705
I can only only write
music, and find music

00:05:02.705 --> 00:05:05.041
that affects me in some way,

00:05:05.041 --> 00:05:08.750
or triggers a memory or
story in my own head.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:10.948
Being as we're all
human, you like to assume

00:05:10.948 --> 00:05:12.881
that if you've been there,
and you've gone through it,

00:05:12.881 --> 00:05:14.098
other people will too.

00:05:14.098 --> 00:05:16.447
I think the only time
when you miss the mark

00:05:16.447 --> 00:05:19.391
is when you sing stuff
that's dishonest for you,

00:05:19.391 --> 00:05:21.568
thinking that, oh, you know
what, I'm not really feeling it,

00:05:21.568 --> 00:05:23.664
but I'm sure a whole lot
of other people will,

00:05:23.664 --> 00:05:25.089
and they're onto you, you know.

00:05:25.089 --> 00:05:27.756
It's one of those things where

00:05:27.756 --> 00:05:29.290
if I find a song or write a song,

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:32.231
even if it feels like it's
more of a general message

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:34.803
or universal message,
sometimes those are the songs

00:05:34.803 --> 00:05:36.485
that need to be sung.

00:05:36.485 --> 00:05:37.738
- It's been said many times,

00:05:37.738 --> 00:05:40.842
perhaps first by a Roman philosopher.

00:05:40.842 --> 00:05:42.049
That

00:05:42.049 --> 00:05:43.547
where

00:05:43.547 --> 00:05:45.244
preparation meets opportunity

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:46.954
is what some people call luck
- Is luck, yeah.

00:05:46.954 --> 00:05:49.421
- And, what's interesting is that

00:05:49.421 --> 00:05:52.297
I definitely had the opportunities.

00:05:52.297 --> 00:05:56.053
I didn't think the
preparation part was ready.

00:05:56.053 --> 00:05:57.627
Like I said, when I started junior high,

00:05:57.627 --> 00:05:58.941
I was a late bloomer.

00:05:58.941 --> 00:06:01.691
I was 13 going on nine, you know?

00:06:03.274 --> 00:06:05.068
When I think about the fact
that I was really given

00:06:05.068 --> 00:06:08.236
my first push by that first
teacher, that's wonderful.

00:06:08.236 --> 00:06:09.618
That was at 13,

00:06:09.618 --> 00:06:13.278
and I was signed to a
major record deal at 17.

00:06:13.278 --> 00:06:16.233
That's not a lot of time
to figure it all out,

00:06:16.233 --> 00:06:19.165
and to all of a sudden be
thrust on a world stage.

00:06:19.165 --> 00:06:21.566
From first solo to first album.

00:06:21.566 --> 00:06:24.541
So, I had to kind of be a professional

00:06:24.541 --> 00:06:26.542
and a student at the same time,

00:06:26.542 --> 00:06:29.249
and I would say that my first,

00:06:29.249 --> 00:06:31.944
if my fans go back and YouTube
any of those promo clips

00:06:31.944 --> 00:06:34.919
from my first record, you
can see behind the eyes

00:06:34.919 --> 00:06:36.611
that I am absolutely terrified.

00:06:36.611 --> 00:06:38.719
There is a major fake it till you make it

00:06:38.719 --> 00:06:40.035
psychology going on there.

00:06:40.035 --> 00:06:41.618
- Well, but fear can be a great motivator.

00:06:41.618 --> 00:06:43.290
- It can, it absolutely can,

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:45.868
and I think that sometimes,
when you're in this business

00:06:45.868 --> 00:06:47.814
for long enough, and it starts to feel

00:06:47.814 --> 00:06:49.269
like another day at the office,

00:06:49.269 --> 00:06:50.958
you actually need to actively search out

00:06:50.958 --> 00:06:53.197
where the fear came
from and find it again.

00:06:53.197 --> 00:06:54.570
That's where the risk-taking happens.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:56.085
That's where deciding
to do a Broadway record

00:06:56.085 --> 00:06:57.411
out of nowhere happens.

00:06:57.411 --> 00:06:59.818
Is what can I do to make me scared again?

00:06:59.818 --> 00:07:01.337
And it can be good.

00:07:01.337 --> 00:07:02.384
When that first record came out,

00:07:02.384 --> 00:07:03.944
we weren't writing it for radio.

00:07:03.944 --> 00:07:05.331
We weren't producing it, really for any,

00:07:05.331 --> 00:07:06.939
even for our label at the time.

00:07:06.939 --> 00:07:08.419
You know, we didn't know what,

00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:09.699
if we could break even,

00:07:09.699 --> 00:07:12.050
if we could sell maybe 50, 60,000 copies,

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:13.018
we will have had a nice run,

00:07:13.018 --> 00:07:17.301
and in some ways, that
vision hasn't changed at all

00:07:17.301 --> 00:07:18.593
in the making of the next six records.

00:07:18.593 --> 00:07:20.917
It was really simple, which was that

00:07:20.917 --> 00:07:23.418
David and I had a really great connection.

00:07:23.418 --> 00:07:26.757
He knew what my voice
did best at the time,

00:07:26.757 --> 00:07:29.578
and I wanted to move people with singing.

00:07:29.578 --> 00:07:31.373
I just wanted to find music,

00:07:31.373 --> 00:07:33.498
or write music, which I didn't
wind up doing till later,

00:07:33.498 --> 00:07:35.813
that made me feel, made other people feel

00:07:35.813 --> 00:07:38.034
the way I felt when I
listened to great singers

00:07:38.034 --> 00:07:39.610
that I really appreciated,
and listened to music

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:40.443
that I appreciated.

00:07:40.443 --> 00:07:43.451
I grew up going to the LA Music Center.

00:07:43.451 --> 00:07:44.941
My parents brought me to
a lot of great things,

00:07:44.941 --> 00:07:46.868
and I would walk out of there with chills,

00:07:46.868 --> 00:07:48.863
just feeling very different
than the ordinary fan.

00:07:48.863 --> 00:07:50.827
I wasn't just humming
it and wearing the hat.

00:07:50.827 --> 00:07:53.697
I was, I wanted to be
that vessel at some point.

00:07:53.697 --> 00:07:55.404
When things start being handed to you.

00:07:55.404 --> 00:07:57.777
When you start getting mass media,

00:07:57.777 --> 00:08:00.270
and things start becoming a little easier,

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:02.301
in terms of getting attention.

00:08:02.301 --> 00:08:04.644
The risk factor involved, and the thing

00:08:04.644 --> 00:08:06.035
that you have to really watch out for,

00:08:06.035 --> 00:08:07.034
and I think it's the reason why there's

00:08:07.034 --> 00:08:07.880
a lot of music out there.

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:10.013
After a while, but just
for whatever reason,

00:08:10.013 --> 00:08:10.847
you can't put your finger on it,

00:08:10.847 --> 00:08:12.341
but it's just not as
inspiring to you anymore

00:08:12.341 --> 00:08:13.556
is when it started.

00:08:13.556 --> 00:08:15.215
It's just that it's easy
to kind of rest on that,

00:08:15.215 --> 00:08:17.145
and one of the things
that I've really wanted to

00:08:17.145 --> 00:08:19.543
pay homage to the success with

00:08:19.543 --> 00:08:21.537
is to not keep the security
blanket around you.

00:08:21.537 --> 00:08:23.677
If you feel like what you're
doing is becoming a formula,

00:08:23.677 --> 00:08:24.510
break the formula.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.123
Try something that
hasn't been done before,

00:08:26.123 --> 00:08:28.019
and work with people and try new things

00:08:28.019 --> 00:08:30.480
that really put the opportunity
out there for failure,

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:31.438
and I think that when you do that,

00:08:31.438 --> 00:08:33.107
that's when the most
exciting things happen.

00:08:33.107 --> 00:08:34.118
When I lost my voice,

00:08:34.118 --> 00:08:35.438
]it had nothing to do with those things.

00:08:35.438 --> 00:08:37.562
It had nothing to do with having
had a bit too much to drink

00:08:37.562 --> 00:08:39.460
or anything like that.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:41.852
I had an allergic reaction

00:08:41.852 --> 00:08:44.378
to something that I ate beforehand,

00:08:44.378 --> 00:08:45.211
and

00:08:46.373 --> 00:08:47.630
my warmups were perfect,

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:49.810
my soundcheck was flawless.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.859
I went out there, and after my third song,

00:08:51.859 --> 00:08:54.040
half of my voice was a whisper.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:55.106
- Where was this, and when was it?

00:08:55.106 --> 00:08:56.030
- Milwaukee.

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:57.060
I'll never forget it.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:58.580
Probably

00:08:58.580 --> 00:08:59.439
2005,

00:08:59.439 --> 00:09:00.272
maybe?

00:09:00.272 --> 00:09:01.669
- So, you're on stage--

00:09:01.669 --> 00:09:05.423
- I'm on stage in front of 18,000 people,

00:09:05.423 --> 00:09:06.590
and I remember

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:11.000
there's no worse feeling than just,

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.515
this wasn't just, oh you've
worked your voice too much,

00:09:14.515 --> 00:09:16.972
oh, you're having trouble
hittin' that note,

00:09:16.972 --> 00:09:18.066
you're just a little tired.

00:09:18.066 --> 00:09:19.801
This was la la la la la,

00:09:19.801 --> 00:09:21.877
la la la la la la la la la la

00:09:21.877 --> 00:09:22.996
la la la la,

00:09:22.996 --> 00:09:25.314
and went, oh, okay, somethin's up.

00:09:25.314 --> 00:09:27.583
Something is wrong here,
because the rest of the day,

00:09:27.583 --> 00:09:29.676
it was just fine.

00:09:29.676 --> 00:09:32.012
So, it was probably the scariest,

00:09:32.012 --> 00:09:34.412
one of the scariest
things I ever had to do

00:09:34.412 --> 00:09:37.436
was level with 18,000 people,

00:09:37.436 --> 00:09:40.436
and say, "Alright, here's what's up.

00:09:41.661 --> 00:09:43.095
"I've got a frog in my throat tonight,

00:09:43.095 --> 00:09:45.068
"I think I'm catching something,

00:09:45.068 --> 00:09:45.901
"and,

00:09:47.246 --> 00:09:49.259
"I'm going to take 15.".

00:09:49.259 --> 00:09:53.020
I said, "I've got a couple
of options here tonight.

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:54.786
"I can lipsynch the rest of the show.".

00:09:54.786 --> 00:09:55.963
"No, boo!"

00:09:55.963 --> 00:09:58.368
"Okay, alright, we won't do that, alright.

00:09:58.368 --> 00:10:01.998
"I can cancel the show
after three songs.",

00:10:01.998 --> 00:10:03.064
"Boo, no, no",

00:10:03.064 --> 00:10:05.992
"Okay, alright, well I don't
want to do that either.

00:10:05.992 --> 00:10:07.286
"Or I could take few minutes,

00:10:07.286 --> 00:10:08.804
"figure out what the heck is going on,

00:10:08.804 --> 00:10:11.344
"and come back and finish
the show for you guys."

00:10:11.344 --> 00:10:12.441
"Yay!"

00:10:12.441 --> 00:10:14.750
"Alright, thanks,
Milwaukee, we'll be back.",

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:16.306
and I walked offstage,

00:10:16.306 --> 00:10:17.139
and

00:10:17.139 --> 00:10:18.472
I

00:10:18.472 --> 00:10:19.887
worked with my voice coach a little bit,

00:10:19.887 --> 00:10:22.742
who thankfully was on the
road with me at that time,

00:10:22.742 --> 00:10:24.505
and flushed my system out with water,

00:10:24.505 --> 00:10:26.869
and just had tea and water.
- Hydrate your body.

00:10:26.869 --> 00:10:28.568
- Hydrated like crazy,

00:10:28.568 --> 00:10:31.151
and through really slow scales,

00:10:32.385 --> 00:10:33.993
and lots and lots of water,

00:10:33.993 --> 00:10:35.076
I was able to

00:10:35.999 --> 00:10:37.814
tightrope-walk

00:10:37.814 --> 00:10:40.440
those notes back into
my throat that night.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:42.826
Then, the thing that
really was the struggle,

00:10:42.826 --> 00:10:45.238
more than the vocal, was
getting my head back that night.

00:10:45.238 --> 00:10:46.071
Having the confidence,

00:10:46.071 --> 00:10:49.160
because once it's not there for you,

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:51.344
there's so many similarities
in singing psychology

00:10:51.344 --> 00:10:53.866
to sports psychology when
I watch a figure skater

00:10:53.866 --> 00:10:55.596
who's just fallen, for instance,

00:10:55.596 --> 00:10:57.881
and then they have to
go and do a triple axel

00:10:57.881 --> 00:10:59.233
five seconds later.

00:10:59.233 --> 00:11:01.383
How do you have the confidence
to land on that knife edge

00:11:01.383 --> 00:11:02.772
after you've just fallen down?

00:11:02.772 --> 00:11:04.230
It's the same kind of thing.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:06.313
And I went out there, and

00:11:07.675 --> 00:11:10.055
the voice got stronger,
and stronger, and stronger.

00:11:10.055 --> 00:11:11.216
People were singing along,

00:11:11.216 --> 00:11:12.065
and by the end of the night,

00:11:12.065 --> 00:11:13.254
I was hittin' the high notes again,

00:11:13.254 --> 00:11:15.043
and I thought, oh god,

00:11:15.043 --> 00:11:17.931
this review is just going to be
the worst review of all time.

00:11:17.931 --> 00:11:19.928
This is just, this is
going to be the worst thing

00:11:19.928 --> 00:11:21.976
anybody's ever read.

00:11:21.976 --> 00:11:22.823
Barely a mention.

00:11:22.823 --> 00:11:24.524
Ah, he felt a little sick,
he left the stage, came back,

00:11:24.524 --> 00:11:26.191
blew everybody away.

00:11:27.107 --> 00:11:28.706
Bullet dodged,

00:11:28.706 --> 00:11:30.187
but that was a great learning experience,

00:11:30.187 --> 00:11:32.567
and something that I would like never

00:11:32.567 --> 00:11:34.947
to have happen again.

00:11:34.947 --> 00:11:37.085
It really is about having
the inner-strength,

00:11:37.085 --> 00:11:38.419
and that just comes from experience,

00:11:38.419 --> 00:11:40.212
to regain your own momentum.

00:11:40.212 --> 00:11:41.401
It's a really nice thing.

00:11:41.401 --> 00:11:42.538
If somebody wants to shake your hands,

00:11:42.538 --> 00:11:45.051
tell you a story about a song
that meant something to them,

00:11:45.051 --> 00:11:48.452
or in one case, there was
a cardiac anesthesiologist

00:11:48.452 --> 00:11:52.177
on the express train, and
she just comes to New York

00:11:52.177 --> 00:11:53.393
for fun.

00:11:53.393 --> 00:11:57.446
She's in DC, and said that I
was the number one requested CD

00:11:57.446 --> 00:11:58.953
for patients who needed to relax

00:11:58.953 --> 00:12:00.256
before they're put under for surgery.

00:12:00.256 --> 00:12:02.427
(audience laughing)

00:12:02.427 --> 00:12:03.844
- That is lovely.

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:08.849
- I mean, that was almost
a compliment, you know?

00:12:08.849 --> 00:12:10.547
(audience laughing)

00:12:10.547 --> 00:12:12.235
- I think that qualifies.
- I think it does too.

00:12:12.235 --> 00:12:13.824
- Yes, it totally does.

00:12:13.824 --> 00:12:14.657
- [NYT Interviewer] I don't the Ramones

00:12:14.657 --> 00:12:15.759
ever got that.
- It puts me right out.

00:12:15.759 --> 00:12:16.592
- Let me tell ya.

00:12:16.592 --> 00:12:18.332
(audience laughing)

00:12:18.332 --> 00:12:19.518
So, it's fascinating.

00:12:19.518 --> 00:12:20.537
I was sad that it was her stop,

00:12:20.537 --> 00:12:22.246
'cause I wanted to talk
to her more, you know?

00:12:22.246 --> 00:12:25.725
But it's cool, I always
meet interesting fans,

00:12:25.725 --> 00:12:29.977
and I would never want
to lose my ability to

00:12:29.977 --> 00:12:31.946
interact with the city.

00:12:31.946 --> 00:12:34.147
- You look like the son

00:12:34.147 --> 00:12:36.913
that the mother, who receives
the CD for Christmas,

00:12:36.913 --> 00:12:38.080
wants to have.

00:12:39.163 --> 00:12:39.996
- Spot on.

00:12:39.996 --> 00:12:41.681
- I think it's bad for families,

00:12:41.681 --> 00:12:43.223
because they then see their own sons

00:12:43.223 --> 00:12:44.270
as less good than you.

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:45.954
(audience laughing)

00:12:45.954 --> 00:12:49.093
I wish my son was a pop
slash opera crossover artist

00:12:49.093 --> 00:12:50.704
rather than working in a call center.

00:12:50.704 --> 00:12:52.251
(audience laughing)

00:12:52.251 --> 00:12:53.421
Anyway, what I'm asking you,

00:12:53.421 --> 00:12:56.356
all I'm asking is that you
look into camera five now,

00:12:56.356 --> 00:12:57.843
and say to sons thinking

00:12:57.843 --> 00:13:00.892
about buying their mothers
Josh Groban albums,

00:13:00.892 --> 00:13:02.698
say don't do it.

00:13:02.698 --> 00:13:04.488
Just talk to your mother and let her know

00:13:04.488 --> 00:13:05.756
that you're a good person, too.

00:13:05.756 --> 00:13:07.871
(audience laughing)

00:13:07.871 --> 00:13:09.297
- Sons and daughters of England,

00:13:09.297 --> 00:13:11.932
(audience laughing)

00:13:11.932 --> 00:13:15.015
I urge you not to purchase my record.

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:19.040
Do something nice, make a
card, buy a puppy for her.

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:22.126
(audience laughing)

00:13:22.126 --> 00:13:24.873
Chocolates, there are
really so many things

00:13:24.873 --> 00:13:26.337
that you can do to make your mother happy

00:13:26.337 --> 00:13:27.609
than buy a Josh Groban CD,

00:13:27.609 --> 00:13:30.609
so please, from Josh Groban, stay away.

00:13:30.609 --> 00:13:33.609
(audience laughing)

00:13:35.149 --> 00:13:36.374
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:36.374 --> 00:13:38.887
I made this video because
YouTuber Peter Hollins

00:13:38.887 --> 00:13:40.071
asked me to.

00:13:40.071 --> 00:13:41.322
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:41.322 --> 00:13:42.992
that you want me to profile next,

00:13:42.992 --> 00:13:44.132
leave it down in the comments below,

00:13:44.132 --> 00:13:45.317
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:45.317 --> 00:13:46.869
I'd also love to know what did Josh say

00:13:46.869 --> 00:13:48.767
that had the biggest
impact on you and why?

00:13:48.767 --> 00:13:50.207
What are you going to take from this video

00:13:50.207 --> 00:13:52.988
and immediately apply to your
life or to your business?

00:13:52.988 --> 00:13:54.199
Leave it in the comments.

00:13:54.199 --> 00:13:55.677
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:13:55.677 --> 00:13:57.155
Finally, I want to give a quick shout out

00:13:57.155 --> 00:13:58.225
to Zeno Nieuwenhuis.

00:13:58.225 --> 00:13:59.919
Thank you so much for picking up a copy

00:13:59.919 --> 00:14:01.604
of my book, Your One Word.

00:14:01.604 --> 00:14:03.928
I really, really, really
appreciate the support,

00:14:03.928 --> 00:14:06.305
and everything you've done
for Believe Nation, as well.

00:14:06.305 --> 00:14:09.388
(soft ambient music)

00:14:16.793 --> 00:14:17.989
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:14:17.989 --> 00:14:19.087
I believe in you.

00:14:19.087 --> 00:14:20.967
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:14:20.967 --> 00:14:24.116
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:14:24.116 --> 00:14:26.207
I'll see you soon.

00:14:26.207 --> 00:14:28.374
(booming)

00:14:31.207 --> 00:14:34.137
- You know, I was a little bit

00:14:34.137 --> 00:14:36.443
not prepared for the edge, I think,

00:14:36.443 --> 00:14:38.386
that high school would
bring at the beginning

00:14:38.386 --> 00:14:40.491
of that period, and so, yeah.

00:14:40.491 --> 00:14:41.598
And when you're a sensitive kid.

00:14:41.598 --> 00:14:44.892
When you're interested in
starting an improv class

00:14:44.892 --> 00:14:47.185
and not joining the football team,

00:14:47.185 --> 00:14:50.267
you know, there's some bullying, too,

00:14:50.267 --> 00:14:53.156
and also, just the
workload gets different.

00:14:53.156 --> 00:14:56.156
I wasn't prepared for the difference

00:14:57.417 --> 00:14:59.305
in academic work.

00:14:59.305 --> 00:15:02.691
It was a lot to take on at once.

00:15:02.691 --> 00:15:04.322
- Well, looking back on it,

00:15:04.322 --> 00:15:06.975
did that have a lasting affect on you?

00:15:06.975 --> 00:15:09.274
- Well, sure, I mean you
have to develop a thick skin.

00:15:09.274 --> 00:15:12.414
You have to find what you're defenses are.

00:15:12.414 --> 00:15:14.513
In my case, back then, it was humor.

00:15:14.513 --> 00:15:15.671
I think that the class clowns

00:15:15.671 --> 00:15:17.049
are always the ones that are feeling

00:15:17.049 --> 00:15:19.898
a little lost and need
to build that shield.

00:15:19.898 --> 00:15:22.629
So, I think that any comedy that I do now

00:15:22.629 --> 00:15:24.717
certainly stems from having

00:15:24.717 --> 00:15:27.292
to fight back in my own way back then,

00:15:27.292 --> 00:15:29.521
and having to deal with my
own insecurities back then.

00:15:29.521 --> 00:15:33.997
I think comedy comes in to
defend that a little bit.

00:15:33.997 --> 00:15:36.339
And also, when looking back on it,

00:15:36.339 --> 00:15:37.638
I wouldn't want to repeat
that time in my life

00:15:37.638 --> 00:15:39.498
for anything in the world,

00:15:39.498 --> 00:15:43.015
but I also know that I learned
so much during that time,

00:15:43.015 --> 00:15:45.787
and I'm glad I didn't peak in high school

00:15:45.787 --> 00:15:46.741
or junior high school.

00:15:46.741 --> 00:15:48.656
I'm glad that I needed to take the time

00:15:48.656 --> 00:15:49.823
to be awkward,

00:15:51.513 --> 00:15:53.427
to figure out that the things

00:15:53.427 --> 00:15:54.260
that were

00:15:57.381 --> 00:16:00.132
considered the cool thing,

00:16:00.132 --> 00:16:01.863
wasn't what I was up to.

00:16:01.863 --> 00:16:04.026
So, okay, what is it that I'm going to do?

00:16:04.026 --> 00:16:05.251
I've got to find my own identity.

00:16:05.251 --> 00:16:06.956
I've got to find what makes me tick.

00:16:06.956 --> 00:16:09.900
I can't rely on throwing the long pass.

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:11.026
I've got to find that thing

00:16:11.026 --> 00:16:12.996
that is going to make me tick.

00:16:12.996 --> 00:16:14.991
It was a music teacher
that finally did it for me.

00:16:14.991 --> 00:16:17.794
It was somebody that said,
"You've got a voice.",

00:16:17.794 --> 00:16:19.135
pulled me out from the back of the choir.

00:16:19.135 --> 00:16:20.261
I was terrified.

00:16:20.261 --> 00:16:22.897
Said, "You're going to sing this song.",

00:16:22.897 --> 00:16:26.231
and after I sang my first solo,

00:16:26.231 --> 00:16:28.141
half of the kids that were at that thing,

00:16:28.141 --> 00:16:29.758
they came up to me and
said, "Man, that was good.

00:16:29.758 --> 00:16:30.623
"Man, that was really good.".

00:16:30.623 --> 00:16:31.974
- You remember what it was, that solo?

00:16:31.974 --> 00:16:34.560
- Yeah, it was 'S Wonderful,
Gershwin's 'S Wonderful.

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:35.666
♫ 'S wonderful

00:16:35.666 --> 00:16:38.249
♫ 'S marvelous

00:16:39.226 --> 00:16:40.292
- Yeah, I like that.

00:16:40.292 --> 00:16:42.116
Would you mind doing that again acapella

00:16:42.116 --> 00:16:43.323
and give me a little more of it?

00:16:43.323 --> 00:16:45.809
♫ You should care for me

00:16:45.809 --> 00:16:47.756
♫ 'S awful nice

00:16:47.756 --> 00:16:49.929
♫ 'S paradise

00:16:49.929 --> 00:16:53.269
♫ What I long to see

00:16:53.269 --> 00:16:56.836
♫ You've made my life so glamorous

00:16:56.836 --> 00:16:58.312
♫ You can't

00:16:58.312 --> 00:16:59.832
- Oh, what is it?

00:16:59.832 --> 00:17:01.944
♫ Me for feeling amorous

00:17:01.944 --> 00:17:03.392
♫ 'S wonderful,

00:17:03.392 --> 00:17:05.050
♫ 'S marvelous

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:07.648
♫ That you should care for me

00:17:07.648 --> 00:17:09.731
- Oh, well done.
- Thanks.

00:17:11.455 --> 00:17:12.705
- And you know.

00:17:13.691 --> 00:17:14.722
I mean, I love Gershwin.

00:17:14.722 --> 00:17:16.930
I love those songs,

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:17.763
and it was fun.

00:17:17.763 --> 00:17:18.923
My parents hadn't heard me sing before,

00:17:18.923 --> 00:17:21.468
and they were crying.

00:17:21.468 --> 00:17:24.122
It was my first feeling of,

00:17:24.122 --> 00:17:26.910
okay, this is, yeah, you could do this.

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:28.609
This is your communication.

